WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.410
Common C# security anti-patterns are repeated shortcuts that become familiar.

2
00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:08.080
The replacement is not one heroic fix.

3
00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:13.771
It is a set of secure habits that make the safe path easier to review and repeat.

4
00:00:13.771 --> 00:00:18.056
Keep secrets out of code, logs, artifacts, and client assets.

5
00:00:18.056 --> 00:00:27.331
Use controlled configuration, environment separation, managed identity where appropriate, secret ownership, rotation, and redaction.

6
00:00:27.331 --> 00:00:30.914
Treat configuration as part of the security design.

7
00:00:30.914 --> 00:00:39.275
Use parameterized data access, review dynamic query behavior, authorize on the server, and validate resource ownership.

8
00:00:39.275 --> 00:00:46.792
Shape input and output with DTOs, safe encoders, controlled serialization, and explicit response contracts.

9
00:00:46.792 --> 00:00:51.078
Avoid dangerous deserialization and untrusted type selection.

10
00:00:51.078 --> 00:00:56.277
Protect tokens, cookies, API keys, and credentials across their lifecycle.

11
00:00:56.277 --> 00:01:05.411
Validate certificates, configure outbound clients deliberately, and design timeouts, retries, and cancellation for external calls.

12
00:01:05.411 --> 00:01:07.518
Finally, make operations safe.

13
00:01:07.518 --> 00:01:21.219
Redact diagnostics, avoid sensitive operational exposure, review production settings, and turn recurring mistakes into checklists, templates, analyzers, pull request prompts, and secure defaults.

14
00:01:21.219 --> 00:01:26.067
The best anti-pattern replacement is a habit the team can keep using.
